<TopicPage slug="subjects/political-science/intermediate/international-relations-theories">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    International Relations (IR) asks <strong>why states (and others) do what they do on the world
    stage</strong> — war, trade, treaties, institutions. You already know domestic politics: there is a
    government, police enforce the law, courts settle disputes. IR is the study of politics
    <em>without</em> that furniture. No world police, no world court with real teeth, no world
    government. About 195 sovereign states coexist in what theorists call a condition of
    <strong>anarchy</strong> — not chaos, but the absence of a common ruler above them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Into that gap three grand theories have moved, and each answers the same questions
    differently: <em>what do states want, and what stops them fighting?</em> Realism says power;
    liberalism says institutions and interdependence; constructivism says ideas. None is a
    religion to convert to — each explains some events brilliantly and others badly, which is
    why professionals carry all three, the way the literature lesson taught you to carry more
    than one reading of a text.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the three grand theories and the critics" widget="equationStep" config={irCfg} />

  <h2>Realism: anarchy, power, self-help</h2>
  <p>
    Realism's core mechanism is simple and ruthless. Because there is no world government to
    call when things go wrong, each state must ultimately guarantee its own survival —
    <strong>self-help</strong>. And because one state's self-defence looks like preparation for
    attack to its neighbours, every state faces the <strong>security dilemma</strong> (identified by
    John Herz in 1950): steps taken to feel safer make rivals feel less safe, producing arms
    races that nobody wanted. Thucydides saw it in 411 BCE, when Athenian envoys told the
    Melians that "the strong do what they can and the weak suffer what they must"; Hobbes saw
    it in the state of nature; E.H. Carr made it the orthodoxy of the 1930s after the
    League of Nations failed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Modern realism comes in two versions. Kenneth Waltz's <em>structural realism</em>
    (1979) argues that outcomes are driven by the shape of the system — how many great powers
    exist (the <strong>polarity</strong>) — more than by leaders' personalities. John Mearsheimer's
    <em>offensive realism</em> (2001) goes further: great powers do not merely defend what they
    have, they seek regional dominance because the safest amount of power is more of it.
    A realist worked example: after 1945, neither Washington nor Moscow was unusually wicked,
    yet each built nuclear arsenals aimed at the other, because in self-help logic the
    alternative — trust — seemed like suicide. Realists also predict <strong>balancing</strong>:
    weaker states gang up against whoever is strongest, which is roughly what NATO (founded
    1949) did to the Soviet Union.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> realism is not "realists love war". It is a theory about
    constraints, not preferences: it claims that <em>even decent states</em> are forced into
    competition by the absence of anyone above them, and that moral crusades often end in
    disaster (Carr's point about interwar idealism).
  </p>

  <h2>Liberalism: institutions, interdependence, democracy</h2>
  <p>
    Liberalism accepts that anarchy exists but denies it is destiny. Its mechanism: cooperation
    pays, and cooperation can be made enforceable. Immanuel Kant's essay <em>Perpetual Peace</em>
    (1795) predicted that republics, commercial interdependence and a federation of states
    would together pacify international life. Two centuries later the evidence is striking.
    <strong>Trade</strong> ties invasion to self-harm: roughly 30 per cent of world GDP now crosses a
    border each year, against about 14 per cent in 1970, and the containerisation of shipping
    (from Malcolm McLean's first container ship in 1956) made distance between allies and
    markets almost free. <strong>Institutions</strong> — the UN (1945), GATT and its successor the WTO
    (1995), the IMF and World Bank (both born at Bretton Woods in 1944) — lower the cost of
    cooperation by providing information, monitoring and repeated dealings, which is how
    game-theory problems like the prisoner's dilemma get softened in practice.
  </p>
  <p>
    The strongest liberal result is the <strong>democratic peace</strong>: mature democracies almost
    never fight full wars against one another — Michael Doyle revived Kant's claim in 1983 and
    Bruce Russett tested it across every pair of states for two centuries. Democracies fight
    plenty of wars with non-democracies; the restraint is specific to the dyad. Why?
    Suggested mechanisms: leaders answer to voters who pay the costs; democratic transparency
    makes threats credible; shared norms make compromise possible.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "liberalism thinks states are good." No — it thinks
    <em>incentives and institutions</em> can tame states of any character, the way domestic courts
    bind domestic bullies. The theory is about structure, not niceness.
  </p>

  <h2>Constructivism: ideas, norms, identity</h2>
  <p>
    Constructivism's move is to ask what both older theories assume away: <em>what does "power"
    or "threat" even mean?</em> Alexander Wendt's article "Anarchy Is What States Make of It"
    (1992) argued that anarchy has no fixed meaning — Britain with hundreds of nuclear warheads
    barely registers as a threat to Washington, while North Korea with a few dozen does. The
    difference is not material capability but <strong>identity and shared history</strong>. States
    can be enemies (rivalry, fear), rivals (competition, rules) or friends (the EU's members),
    and which one they become is built by practice and talk.
  </p>
  <p>
    Constructivism has concrete wins. Why did NATO survive the dissolution of the Warsaw Pact
    in 1991 — the realist puzzle Mearsheimer flagged in 1990? Partly because the alliance had
    become an identity, not just a shield. Why did states ban anti-personnel landmines in the
    Ottawa Treaty (1997), and why has no state used a nuclear weapon in war since 1945? Not
    because interests changed — the weapons still work — but because a <strong>norm taboo</strong>
    formed (Nina Tannenwald's "nuclear taboo"). Material facts get their meaning from norms:
    that is the constructivist's claim, and it is hard to deny once you notice how much
    politics is argument about legitimacy.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="applying the lenses to the biggest live question in world politics" widget="equationStep" config={irAppCfg} />

  <h2>The state and globalisation</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus's first topic asks who the actors even are. The <strong>state</strong> — a
    government ruling a territory and population, recognised by other states (sovereignty,
    dating to the Peace of Westphalia, 1648) — remains the only actor with a passport office
    and an army. But globalisation stretches and deepens connections across borders: finance,
    production chains, data, climate, pandemics. A smartphone is designed in one country, built
    from components made in a dozen, assembled in another and sold everywhere — no single state
    controls it. The realist reply is that states are still the top of the food chain (they can
    tax, conscript and print money; firms cannot). The liberal and constructivist reply is that
    sovereignty has always been a bundle of practices, not a wall, and the bundle is being
    renegotiated — over data rules, tax havens and vaccine patents — by actors like the EU,
    Apple, Greenpeace and the WHO, none of which existed in Westphalia's world.
  </p>

  <h2>Global governance: political, economic, human rights and environmental</h2>
  <p>
    Without world government there is <strong>global governance</strong>: institutions that do
    coordination without sovereignty. The UN Security Council (15 members, 5 permanent with
    vetoes: US, UK, France, Russia, China) can authorise force and sanctions but is frozen by
    the P5 veto — a realist institution at its core. The UN's ~70,000 peacekeepers patrol
    ceasefire lines rather than win wars. Economically, the WTO administers trade rules and
    dispute settlement (states mostly comply with rulings against them — a liberal's favourite
    fact); the IMF lends to countries in balance-of-payments crises, usually with conditions;
    the World Bank funds development.
  </p>
  <p>
    Human rights governance runs through treaties — the Universal Declaration (1948), the
    Geneva Conventions, the International Criminal Court (opened 2002; the US, Russia, China
    and India never joined) — which try to make how a state treats <em>its own citizens</em> a
    legitimate international concern, a quiet revolution against sovereignty. Environmental
    governance faces the hardest structure: a genuinely global commons problem, where each
    state's incentive is to let others cut emissions. The Paris Agreement (2015) chose
    flexibility: nationally chosen pledges reviewed every five years, aiming to hold warming
    well below 2 °C. The theories read it differently: realists see pledges without enforcement
    as cheap talk; liberals see repeated review cycles building cooperation; constructivists
    see a norm of naming-and-shaming that has already shifted what governments dare to promise.
  </p>

  <h2>Power and development</h2>
  <p>
    Power in IR is not just tanks. Joseph Nye distinguished <strong>hard power</strong> (coercion:
    the US military budget, around $900 billion a year, exceeds the next several states
    combined) from <strong>soft power</strong> — getting others to want what you want, through
    culture, universities, aid, the appeal of your model (Hollywood, the Fulbright programme,
    the "rules-based order" itself). <strong>Smart power</strong> combines them. Development, meanwhile,
    changes who holds power: China's share of world output rose from about 2 per cent in 1980
    to roughly 18 per cent today, and the BRICS grouping (Brazil, Russia, India, China, South
    Africa) claims to speak for a "global South" that older institutions under-represent —
    which is why reforming the Security Council and the IMF's voting shares is a live
    diplomatic battle. Theories of development disagree sharply too: modernisation theory says
    growth follows institutions and investment; dependency theory (Prebisch, Frank) says the
    global economy is structured so that the periphery stays peripheral. Whether the
    spectacular catch-up of East Asia vindicates or refutes each is one of the field's
    running arguments.
  </p>

  <h2>Regionalism and the European Union</h2>
  <p>
    Between the state and the world sits <strong>regionalism</strong>: groups of states that pool
    sovereignty partly. The EU is the deepest experiment ever run. It began in 1951 with six
    states pooling coal and steel — war industries, deliberately — then widened step by step:
    a common market (Treaty of Rome, 1957), direct elections (1979), a single currency (the
    euro, 1999–2002, now used by 20 of its 27 members), open borders in the Schengen area.
    Two theories explain it. <strong>Neo-functionalism</strong> (Ernst Haas) predicts
    <em>spillover</em>: integrating one sector creates problems that push integration into the
    next — you cannot run a common market without common rules on standards, then on money,
    then on everything. <strong>Intergovernmentalism</strong> (Andrew Moravcsik) replies that member
    states remain the bosses and only integrate where their interests align. Both are partly
    right, and crises — the euro crisis, Brexit (the first member exit, completed 2020) —
    fuelled both. The EU matters beyond Europe because it is the existence proof that anarchy
    can be engineered away, at least regionally: France and Germany have fought three wars in
    150 years and now share a parliament.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three refinements separate the tourist from the student. First, the
    <strong>level-of-analysis problem</strong> (Waltz's "three images"): war can be explained by human
    nature, by state character, or by the international system — and explanations at different
    levels can conflict without either being wrong. Second, the <strong>English School</strong> sits
    between realism and constructivism: Hedley Bull's "international society" — states bound by
    shared rules (diplomacy, international law, the balance of power) even without a ruler, a
    middle position that fits how diplomats actually talk. Third, the <strong>critical
    lenses</strong>: feminist IR asks where women are in a field obsessed with soldiers and
    statesmen; postcolonial IR asks why "the standard of civilisation" once divided the world
    into rule-makers and rule-takers — questions that change what counts as a fact worth
    explaining.
  </p>
  <p>
    The open questions are the big ones. Does the democratic peace hold as democracies erode
    at home? Will nuclear deterrence keep holding for a 75th year and beyond — the most
    important untested assumption on Earth? Can governance of a shared atmosphere work when
    enforcement is voluntary? And does any theory built for a world of states survive a world
    of networks? IR theory is not settled doctrine; it is the toolkit for arguing about those
    questions on evidence.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kant (1795); Morgenthau, Politics Among Nations (1948); Wendt, "Anarchy Is What States Make of It" (1992); Russett on democratic peace; Waltz (1979); Mearsheimer (2001); Nye on soft power; Allison, Destined for War (2017).</p>
